VII. Combat Sports: Accounting for 50 out of 100 points in human longevity
This represents a recent major shift in Justin Sun's mindset—a "trend" that the internet once mocked:
- Data from AI: Longevity is scored out of 100 points, comprising 25 points for cardiopulmonary health and 25 points for muscle mass (totaling 50 points);
- Practicing combat sports (which includes strength training) allows one to max out both the cardiopulmonary and muscle categories, while also providing the benefit of passive fat burning;
- Muscle is your body's bank account: illness draws from muscle reserves, recovery relies on muscle, and avoiding frailty in old age depends on muscle;
- He asked the AI: "Musk, Buffett, and Trump don't work out—does that mean the 'lean muscle' theory is wrong?" The AI ​​offered a wake-up call: "Does Buffett walk into a restaurant on his own, or is he wheeled in?"
So he asks: When you turn 80, do you want to walk in yourself or be wheeled in? Forty minutes a day in exchange for forty years of quality life—by any calculation, it’s a worthwhile investment.
VIII. The Meaning of Life: Experience over conclusions; create more, consume less
Towards the end of the interview, the conversation turned to the purpose of living.
Sun adopts the mindset of a "player": life is like the game *Earth Online*, and he is still in the exploration phase.
- Spend the first 50% of the journey observing without drawing conclusions; use the second 50% to make judgments once you have a frame of reference;
- Meaning isn't something you think up; it’s something you experience. Setting out on the journey is more important than fully understanding *why* you started;
- When Nobel invented dynamite, he was reviled as a "merchant of death"; yet, by establishing five prizes near the end of his life, he created what became humanity's highest honor—intentions and outcomes often bear no relation to one another;
- There are only two types of human activity: creation and consumption. If you only consume without creating, you are merely spinning your wheels, no matter how wealthy you are.

---
A Final Note: What this interview is really trying to tell you
Condensing four hours into a single sentence: Mindset determines destiny, and AI amplifies that mindset.  At twenty, you have no money, no connections, and no resources—yet this is not a disadvantage, but an asset: unencumbered by baggage, you can instantly "install" the latest theoretical frameworks, whereas successful figures from the previous generation are often locked into outdated mindsets by their own egos.
As Justin Sun frequently says, "The manifestation of your mindset is your wealth and your muscle." You don't need to "become" someone specific first; you simply need to update your understanding of the world and let AI help you turn your ideas into tangible output.
